# Queens Closet — Project Rules

Queens Closet is a premium AI-powered virtual fashion studio: upload clothing designs and client
photos, generate AI virtual try-on previews, and manage generated looks. It must feel like a
premium fashion-tech product, never a generic CRUD admin dashboard.

## Stack

Next.js (App Router) + React + TypeScript (strict) + Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui + Lucide React +
Prisma + Neon Postgres + Auth.js (Credentials, JWT sessions) + Zod + React Hook Form +
Vercel Blob (image storage) + FASHN AI (virtual try-on) + Vercel deployment.
Do not introduce alternative frameworks/libraries for things already covered here without asking.

## TypeScript

No `any`, no unnecessary type assertions, no duplicated interfaces, no untyped API responses.
Prefer explicit types/interfaces, discriminated unions, Zod schemas, and typed service functions.

## Architecture (never skip layers)

```
UI (app/**, components/**)
  -> lib/services/*  (business logic, ownership checks)
    -> lib/db/prisma.ts        (Prisma access)
    -> lib/ai/*                (VirtualTryOnProvider interface + provider impls)
    -> lib/storage/*           (ImageStorage interface + provider impls)
```

Never put business logic, Prisma calls, or provider API calls directly inside React components or
route handlers — call a service function instead.

### AI provider abstraction

All AI calls go through `VirtualTryOnProvider` (`lib/ai/virtual-try-on-provider.ts`): `createGeneration`,
`getStatus`, `getResult`, optional `cancel`, and normalized provider errors. `lib/ai/index.ts` selects
the implementation via `AI_PROVIDER` env var. The current implementation targets FASHN AI
(`POST /v1/run`, `GET /v1/status/{id}`). Swapping providers must never require UI changes. Never train
a model or run GPU inference inside the Next.js app/Vercel functions.

### Storage abstraction

All uploads go through `ImageStorage` (`lib/storage/image-storage.ts`) with a Vercel Blob
implementation. Store only URLs + metadata (width, height, format, storage key) in Postgres —
never raw image bytes.

## Generation flow (must follow in order)

1. Validate person image, 2. validate garment image, 3. validate garment category, 4. upload images,
5. create `TryOnSession`, 6. status `QUEUED`, 7. submit to provider, 8. store provider job id,
9. status `PROCESSING`, 10. poll/webhook status, 11. store result, 12. status `COMPLETED`, 13. display result.
On failure: store the error, set status `FAILED`, show a useful user-facing message, never expose
provider secrets or internals.

## Image rules

Validate MIME type, extension, file size, and dimensions. Supported formats: JPG/JPEG/PNG/WEBP only.

## Security & privacy

Never expose DB credentials, AI API keys, storage credentials, or auth secrets to the browser — keep
secrets server-side via env vars. Validate all server inputs with Zod. Authenticate every protected
route and authorize every resource: a user must never access another user's clients, garments, or
generated images (always scope Prisma queries by `userId`). Client photos are sensitive: support
consent status, deletion, and ownership checks. Never use client photos for model training. Never
send images to the AI provider except on an explicit user-triggered generation.

## Database

Use Prisma migrations only — never hand-edit the production schema. Use foreign keys, indexes on
`userId`/status columns, timestamps on major entities, and soft deletion (`archivedAt`) for
catalogue records instead of hard deletes.

## API conventions

Zod-validate all request bodies. Respond with `{ success: true, data }` or
`{ success: false, error: { code, message } }` (see `lib/api/response.ts`). Never return raw
exceptions or stack traces to the client.

## UI direction

Elegant, modern, minimal, editorial, spacious, mobile-responsive, accessible. Avoid generic
admin-dashboard looks, excessive gradients/animations, clutter, unnecessary cards, and inconsistent
spacing. Reuse the existing design system/components instead of inventing new patterns.

## Loading & error states

Every async action (upload, generate, load, delete, save) needs a visible loading state. Errors must
be predictable, logged server-side, and shown as clear, actionable messages (e.g. "We couldn't
generate this look. Please try another photo or garment image.") — never raw `500` text.

## AI cost protection

Disable the Generate button while a request is in flight; guard against duplicate submissions and
rate-limit generation endpoints. Track generation/usage status so retries don't silently multiply
provider calls.

## General behavior

Before large features: inspect existing code, reuse existing components/services, identify affected
files, implement the smallest coherent change, then validate (typecheck, lint, build). Don't rewrite
working code unnecessarily, install packages without reason, or create duplicate
components/utilities. Don't leave TODO placeholders for core functionality.
